[strategy/guidance/application/state-management/GUID-APP-TEC-002-zustand-over-redux-guidance.md#principle-3-phi-never-enters-the-store]

#### Principle 3: PHI Never Enters the Store

**Description:** Neither Zustand nor Redux stores should ever contain PHI. PHI lives exclusively in component state, fetched at runtime from authenticated backend APIs using opaque identifiers.

**Rationale:** HIPAA Security Rule §164.312(a)(1) prohibits PHI in serialized or persistent state. Both Zustand (with `devtools` or `persist` middleware) and Redux DevTools can serialize store state to the browser or to storage. Keeping PHI out of the store entirely is the safest defense.

**Application:**

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT: Zustand store holds only opaque identifiers and UI state
interface ClaimsUIState {
  selectedClaimId: string | null;  // ✅ Opaque ID — not PHI
  filterStatus: 'all' | 'pending' | 'approved' | 'denied'; // ✅ UI state
}

// ❌ WRONG: PHI in Zustand store
interface ClaimsUIState {
  currentMember: {
    name: string;        // ❌ PHI
    ssn: string;         // ❌ PHI — HIPAA violation
    diagnosis: string;   // ❌ PHI
  };
}

// ✅ CORRECT: Fetch PHI in component state using the ID from the store
function ClaimDetail() {
  const selectedClaimId = useClaimsStore(s => s.selectedClaimId);
  const { data: claimData } = useQuery({  // PHI fetched here, in component state
    queryKey: ['claim', selectedClaimId],
    queryFn: () => fetchClaimById(selectedClaimId!),
    enabled: !!selectedClaimId,
  });
  // claimData (PHI) stays in React Query's component-scoped cache
}
```

