#### Best Practice 3: Explicit Cleanup on Module Unmount

**Description:** Every Zustand store in a micro-frontend module must be reset to its initial state when the module unmounts.

**Rationale:** Module Federation loads and unloads modules as users navigate. Without explicit cleanup, stale state (including opaque identifiers that might indirectly point to PHI) persists in memory across navigation events, potentially leaking context across tenant boundaries in multi-tenant environments.

> **Security note:** Client-side store cleanup is a UX hygiene measure — it prevents stale context appearing in the UI after navigation. It is not a security boundary. Tenant data isolation is enforced server-side via the authorization model defined in [STD-SEC-AUTHZ-001 §3.4 Multi-Tenant Authorization](../../../../standards/security/authorization/STD-SEC-AUTHZ-001-authorization-implementation.md). Both controls are required.

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// claims-mfe/src/store/index.ts
export const INITIAL_CLAIMS_UI_STATE: ClaimsUIState = {
  selectedClaimId: null,
  filterStatus: 'all',
  isDetailPanelOpen: false,
};

export const useClaimsUIStore = create<ClaimsUIState>(() => INITIAL_CLAIMS_UI_STATE);

// Export a cleanup function for use in mount/unmount lifecycle
export const resetClaimsUIState = () =>
  useClaimsUIStore.setState(INITIAL_CLAIMS_UI_STATE);

// claims-mfe/src/App.tsx
export function ClaimsMfeMount({ context }: MfeProps) {
  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      resetClaimsUIState();         // ✅ Clear Zustand state
      queryClient.clear();          // ✅ Clear TanStack Query cache (PHI)
    };
  }, []);

  return <ClaimsRoutes />;
}
```

**Success Indicators:** Browser memory profiler shows stable heap after repeated module mount/unmount cycles; no stale `selectedClaimId` values persisting across module navigations.

