### Use Case 2: Shell Application Auth State (Zustand — Correct)

**Scenario Description:** The Next.js shell application needs to hold non-PHI user session state (userId, roles, tenantId, sessionExpiry) accessible across shell layout components.

**Context:** Next.js shell; auth context hydrated server-side from NextAuth.js session; passed to Zustand store on client hydration.

**Guidance Application:**

```typescript
// shell/src/store/authStore.ts
import { create } from 'zustand';

interface AuthState {
  userId: string | null;
  tenantId: string | null;
  roles: string[];
  sessionExpiresAt: number | null;
  setAuth: (auth: Omit<AuthState, 'setAuth' | 'clearAuth'>) => void;
  clearAuth: () => void;
}

export const useAuthStore = create<AuthState>((set) => ({
  userId: null,
  tenantId: null,
  roles: [],
  sessionExpiresAt: null,
  setAuth: (auth) => set(auth),
  clearAuth: () => set({ userId: null, tenantId: null, roles: [], sessionExpiresAt: null }),
}));
// No PHI — userId and tenantId are opaque identifiers
// DevTools NOT enabled — auth state should not be inspectable
```

**Success Factors:** No PHI in store (member names, SSNs are never in auth state); `clearAuth()` called on logout and session timeout; DevTools middleware not added.

